<!-- The release build's manifest, and it exists to take one permission away.

     `mobile_scanner` reads QR codes with ML Kit, and ML Kit brings
     `com.google.android.datatransport:transport-backend-cct` with it —
     Google's own telemetry queue, which declares INTERNET and
     ACCESS_NETWORK_STATE in its library manifest. Nothing in lib/ opens a
     socket, so those permissions arrived in the package without any code in
     this app asking for them, and the merged release manifest carried INTERNET
     for as long as nobody looked.

     That mattered because website/privacy.html says, in bold, that the Android
     release package is built without it, and offers that as the reason the
     claim cannot be quietly reversed in a later build. A privacy page that can
     be falsified with `aapt dump permissions` is worse than no privacy page.
     So: make the sentence true rather than soften it.

     Why the release source set rather than main. A build-type manifest sits
     above main and far above any library in the merger's priority order, which
     is what lets `tools:node="remove"` here delete a declaration a dependency
     made. Putting it in main would also strip the INTERNET that
     src/debug/AndroidManifest.xml and src/profile/AndroidManifest.xml add for
     the Flutter tool's own use — that is how hot reload and the observatory
     talk to a running app, and losing it would break `flutter run` and
     `make android` to fix a problem neither of them has.

     ACCESS_NETWORK_STATE is deliberately left alone. It transmits nothing —
     it reads whether a network exists — and taking it away is not free:
     ConnectivityManager throws SecurityException when asked without it, and
     the thing doing the asking would be inside ML Kit where nothing here can
     catch it. Removing INTERNET already closes the socket; removing the
     read as well would risk the scanner to make a shorter list. -->
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    xmlns:tools="http://schemas.android.com/tools">
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.INTERNET" tools:node="remove" />
</manifest>
